A1. Here's something to kick about (pl.). ~ FOOTBALLS
A8. This may be straight or crooked, but is often on the square. ~ MARK
A9. Don't put this against your house, it's possible that you may lose it. ~ LIEN
A11. These might change the present tendency in women's clothing. ~ MICE
A12. Short measures. ~ ENS
A14. Some women do this with a lipstick. ~ DAUB
A16. A picnic gate-crasher. ~ ANT
A17. When better women are made, this is what men will keep them in. ~ SATIN
A18. The childhood home of Bacon. ~ STY
A19. It's open season on pedestrians here (abbr.). ~ RD
A21. This is sometimes covered with feathers, but it makes a queer-looking bird. ~ TAR
A23. The first letters of simplified spelling. ~ SS
A24. This was a favorite pronoun of H. Rider Haggard. ~ SHE
A26. A great necker. ~ BOA
A28. He lives on the fat of the land. ~ ESKIMO
A29. It's a cinch you can see through this if you're not blind. ~ CORNEA
A30. A woman never brags about these (abbr.). ~ YRS
A32. What every woman is before she marries. ~ NEE
A33. Used extensively in construction of domes, often too freely. ~ OS
A35. Villainous laughter. ~ AHA
A37. We'd like to see this on all our bills (abbr.). ~ PD
A38. This has a red skin even when young and green. ~ UTE
A40. What our last week's salaries are. ~ SPENT
A41. This should be chalked up. ~ CUE
A42. A small beginning. ~ SEED
A44. They do this at a trial. ~ TRY
A45. This comes from contented cats. ~ PURR
A46. This may be guilty or not guilty but not necessarily the truth. ~ PLEA
A48. To the manor born. ~ EARL
A49. These made a hit with the High Hats on Fifth Avenue last winter. ~ SNOWBALLS

D1. This is a stubborn thing. ~ FACT
D2. You'll have to dig for this. ~ ORE
D3. A mark of approval. ~ OK
D4. Small fry. ~ BANTAM
D5. Low life (abbr.). ~ LL
D6. This is off in Chicago. ~ LID
D7. When these are high, your spirits are low. ~ SEAS
D8. Most people have nothing here to brag about. ~ MIND
D10. You'll find lots of these on some family trees. ~ NUTS
D11. I may be wrong, but I think it's wonderful. ~ MARVELOUS
D12. What chorus girls can always do, and how! ~ EAT
D13. Form of address, practically obsolete, which departed with bustles. ~ SIR
D15. According to the newspapers this is always innocent but usually gets it in the neck. ~ BYSTANDER
D20. Some lost this in the stock market, and others in the laundry. ~ SHIRT
D22. We breathe through these. ~ PORES
D24. This is the limit. ~ SKY
D25. Found at the printers'. ~ EMS
D26. Two of these make something to reach for instead of a Lucky. ~ BON
D27. Something odd in Scotland. ~ ANE
D31. The little brat next door, according to his dear, dear mother. ~ CHERUB
D34. This was taken up to the Capitol last week. ~ STEP
D35. What a prodigy is. ~ APT
D36. This port in a storm. ~ ANY
D37. What the Prince of Wales will do if he does any more knitting. ~ PURL
D39. As slippery as greased pigs at the county fair. ~ EELS
D41. Vagrant offspring of the most casual amours. ~ CURS
D43. Lair of the family beast of burden. ~ DEN
D45. Dear to a boy's heart. ~ PAL
D47. Army Orders (abbr.). ~ AO
D48. This loops the loop in Chicago. ~ EL


